-- Carbon-credit provenance: no green claim should become official
-- without admissible provenance.
--
-- A green claim without admissible provenance is not a weak green
-- claim. It is not a green claim at all.
--
-- A quick tour of the domain, in case carbon markets are not your world.
-- A carbon credit certifies a quantity of climate benefit - tonnes of
-- carbon-dioxide equivalent avoided, reduced, or removed - traceable to a
-- verified measurement. (Registries often standardise one credit to one
-- tonne; here a credit carries its quantity explicitly, so nothing below
-- turns on that convention.) What makes a credit valuable is not the
-- arithmetic behind it but its *standing*: that a
-- registry, buyer, auditor, or regulator can later justify it - who
-- verified the measurement, who attested it, who was accredited at the
-- time, who holds it now, and whether it has already been retired
-- (used up). A credit no one can justify after the fact is not a small
-- problem; it is a liability.
--
-- The measurement itself - generation times an emission factor, the
-- monitoring-and-verification ("MRV") maths - is the meter. Morpholog
-- does not compute it. That stays outside, and comes back as an admitted
-- claim that a measurement has been verified to some quantity. Morpholog
-- governs only the admission of standing: verified, attested, issued,
-- held, retired.
--
-- Morpholog does not store "facts" - it stores *claims* it has agreed to
-- admit because they obey the rules. Two kinds of rule do the work:
--
--   * an `invariant` is a rule that must hold for all admitted state,
--     forever. The runtime refuses to commit any change that would break
--     one - this is how an impossible state (two credits for the same
--     measurement, one credit held in two places) becomes uncommittable.
--   * a `require` is a gate on a single action, checked at the moment you
--     act and never again. "Only an accredited verifier may attest" is a
--     gate; "no two credits may back the same measurement" is an
--     invariant. A gate governs what you may do next; an invariant
--     governs what is true.
--
-- Currentness matters here, and the two kinds of rule make it precise.
-- Revoking a verifier's accreditation blocks *new* issuance through them
-- from that moment on - but it does not reach back and erase credits that
-- were legitimately issued while they were accredited. What was admitted
-- stays admitted; only what you may do next changes.
--
-- Finally, some rules are about time. A scheme may oblige an account to
-- retire enough credits by a deadline. Morpholog keeps no clock of its
-- own, and that is deliberate: "now" is known only outside the system,
-- so a deadline is
-- only ever checked when an outside scheduler hands the current date to a
-- `sweep` transformation. The kernel decides; the coordinator says when.

program carbon_credit_provenance

-- The vocabulary. Each `predicate` names a kind of claim this world can
-- hold. `VerifiedMeasurement` carries the quantity the meter reported;
-- admitting it records "this measurement was verified to this amount",
-- never "this is the truth". `Attestation(measurement, verifier)` means
-- the verifier attests the admitted measurement claim as a whole,
-- including its quantity.
predicate Accredited(verifier: Subject)
-- A measurement is verified to one quantity, not several - declared
-- on the predicate, which also keeps the amount a credit inherits
-- at issuance unambiguous.
predicate VerifiedMeasurement(measurement: Subject, quantity: Decimal)
    unique by (measurement)
predicate Attestation(measurement: Subject, verifier: Subject)
-- Two declared uniqueness commitments carry the no-double-counting
-- doctrine: at most one credit per measurement (two credits backed
-- by one measurement is the canonical double-counting failure), and
-- one measurement per credit - so credits and measurements line up
-- one to one.
predicate Issued(credit: Subject, measurement: Subject, quantity: Decimal)
    unique by (measurement)
    unique by (credit)
-- Custody is a retractable pointer: a credit is in one place at a
-- time, and a transfer moves the pointer.
predicate HeldBy(credit: Subject, account: Subject)
    current pointer by (credit)
predicate Retired(credit: Subject, account: Subject)
-- A compliance obligation: `account` must retire `quantity` tonnes of
-- credits by `due_on`. Its outcome is recorded as satisfied or breached.
-- An obligation has one set of terms; the discharge and sweep
-- transformations rely on the lookup being unambiguous.
predicate Obligation(obligation: Subject, account: Subject, quantity: Decimal, due_on: Date)
    unique by (obligation)
predicate ObligationSatisfied(obligation: Subject)
predicate ObligationBreached(obligation: Subject)

-- The states this model makes impossible, forever.

-- Retirement is terminal: no credit is ever both retired and held.
-- Retiring a credit retracts its custody, so a retired credit can never
-- be transferred or retired again.
invariant retirement_terminal:
    not (Retired(credit, ra) and HeldBy(credit, ha))

-- An obligation is never both satisfied and breached at once.
invariant obligation_not_both_satisfied_and_breached:
    not (ObligationSatisfied(o) and ObligationBreached(o))

-- The only ways state may change.

-- Accredit a verifier. `revoke_accreditation` retracts that standing;
-- from then on the verifier may not attest, and may not back new
-- issuance - but credits already issued are left untouched.
transformation grant_accreditation(verifier):
    admit Accredited(verifier)

transformation revoke_accreditation(verifier):
    require Accredited(verifier)
    retract Accredited(verifier)

-- Admit the MRV result as evidence. The quantity was computed outside
-- Morpholog; here we only record that the measurement has been verified
-- to it.
transformation verify_measurement(measurement, quantity):
    admit VerifiedMeasurement(measurement, quantity)

-- Only a currently accredited verifier may attest a measurement.
transformation attest_measurement(measurement, verifier):
    require Accredited(verifier)
    admit Attestation(measurement, verifier)

-- Issue a credit. This is the legitimacy gate: the measurement must be
-- verified (which binds the quantity the credit will carry), and a
-- currently accredited verifier must have attested it. If any link is
-- missing, the runtime refuses - and can say exactly which claim is
-- absent and which transformation would supply it.
transformation issue_credit(credit, measurement, verifier, account):
    bind VerifiedMeasurement(measurement, quantity)
    require Attestation(measurement, verifier) and Accredited(verifier)
    admit Issued(credit, measurement, quantity)
    admit HeldBy(credit, account)

-- Move a credit between accounts. A retired credit cannot move, and you
-- may transfer only a credit you currently hold.
transformation transfer_credit(credit, from_account, to_account):
    require not Retired(credit, _)
    require HeldBy(credit, from_account)
    retract HeldBy(credit, from_account)
    admit HeldBy(credit, to_account)

-- Retire (cancel) a credit against an account. Terminal: the gate refuses
-- a second retirement, and retracting custody keeps the
-- retirement-terminal invariant true.
transformation retire_credit(credit, account):
    require not Retired(credit, _)
    require HeldBy(credit, account)
    retract HeldBy(credit, account)
    admit Retired(credit, account)

-- Obligations over time. A scheme raises one; retirement discharges it;
-- and an outside scheduler's sweep records a breach once the deadline has
-- passed. "Now" is the `current_date` the sweep is handed, never a clock
-- inside the kernel.

transformation raise_obligation(obligation, account, quantity, due_on):
    admit Obligation(obligation, account, quantity, due_on)

-- Discharge on or before the deadline, once the account has retired
-- enough. Like the sweep, discharge is date-aware - `current_date` comes
-- from the outside coordinator - so a late retirement cannot quietly
-- satisfy a "by due_on" obligation. An already-decided obligation cannot
-- be discharged again.
transformation discharge_obligation(obligation, current_date):
    bind Obligation(obligation, account, quantity, due_on)
    require not ObligationSatisfied(obligation)
    require not ObligationBreached(obligation)
    require current_date on_or_before due_on
    require sum(q | Retired(c, account) and Issued(c, m, q)) >= quantity
    admit ObligationSatisfied(obligation)

-- The outside-coordinator sweep. Past the due date, not already decided,
-- and still under target: record the breach.
transformation sweep_obligation(obligation, current_date):
    bind Obligation(obligation, account, quantity, due_on)
    require not ObligationSatisfied(obligation)
    require not ObligationBreached(obligation)
    require current_date after due_on
    require sum(q | Retired(c, account) and Issued(c, m, q)) < quantity
    admit ObligationBreached(obligation)
